// Emperor Japanese Maple — Acer palmatum 'Emperor I' (Emperor One)
// Edit this file to update plant-specific content.

#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Emperor Japanese Maple", "Acer palmatum 'Emperor I' (Emperor One)", "japanese_maple")[
  Emperor I is one of the most sun-tolerant Japanese maple cultivars available, featuring striking deep burgundy-red foliage that holds its color through summer heat — unlike many cultivars that fade to green. It leafs out later than most Japanese maples, which helps it avoid late spring freezes. Mature size: 12–15 ft tall, 10 ft wide, slow to moderate growth. Despite its relatively good sun tolerance, significant afternoon sun in Commerce City's high-altitude, low-humidity, intensely bright conditions is a real stress factor — this tree is currently struggling and needs targeted establishment support.
]

=== Watering

This tree is on drip irrigation. During establishment (which may extend 3–5 years given current stress), water more aggressively than the other trees. Target 8–12 gallons per watering event, 3 times per week in June–August during heat events. For 1 gph emitters, run the zone 60–90 minutes. Japanese maples are shallow-rooted and cannot access deep moisture reserves — they feel drought stress quickly. Key signs of water stress: leaf margins turn brown and crispy, leaves cup downward, or the tree drops leaves prematurely in summer. If you see these signs, hand-water deeply immediately in addition to the drip cycle. In spring and fall, reduce to 2x per week. Do not let the root zone dry out completely in any season.

=== Fertilization

Emperor I prefers slightly acidic soil (pH 5.5–6.5) — your soil at pH 7.27 is on the alkaline side for this plant, which contributes to its struggle. Do NOT use standard balanced fertilizer, especially nothing with phosphorus (your soil P is HIGH). In early spring, apply a light application of an acidifying fertilizer such as #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=hollytone+fertilizer+japanese+maple")[Hollytone] (4-3-4 with sulfur) or ammonium sulfate (21-0-0) — ammonium sulfate acidifies slightly over time. Apply a chelated micronutrient drench in May, using EDDHA chelated iron specifically — it works best in alkaline soil. Annual acidic organic mulch (pine needle mulch or pine bark) will gradually lower pH in the root zone over years. Do not fertilize after mid-July.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Japanese maples rarely need pruning and are often harmed by over-pruning. Prune in late winter (February) or in summer (July–August) when cuts heal fastest. Focus only on removing dead wood, crossing branches, or branches rubbing the structure. Do not remove more than 1/4 of the canopy in a single season — this stresses an already-struggling tree further. Over several years, you can selectively remove lower branches to raise the canopy if desired. Always use sharp, sterilized tools.

=== Wildlife Value

Emperor Japanese Maple provides modest direct wildlife value but contributes to garden diversity. Butterflies and native bees occasionally visit the inconspicuous spring flowers. The layered, low branching structure provides cover and shelter for house finches and other small birds. Cottontail rabbits may chew the bark of young or stressed trunks, especially in winter — protect with a hardware cloth cylinder (18" tall) from November through March. Field mice can also gnaw bark at the base; keep mulch several inches from the trunk at all times.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+leaf+scorch+treatment")[Leaf scorch] — brown, crispy leaf margins — is the most significant issue for this tree in your conditions and is primarily caused by afternoon sun combined with inadequate moisture or root stress. It is not a pathogen; address by increasing watering frequency and providing afternoon shade relief (see Other Care). #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+verticillium+wilt")[Verticillium wilt] causes sudden wilting or death of individual branches — prune affected wood 12" below symptoms with sterilized tools; no systemic cure. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+aphids")[Aphids] may appear on new growth in spring — treat with insecticidal soap. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+maple+scale+insects+treatment")[Scale insects] can colonize stressed bark; treat with horticultural oil in early spring before leaf-out. A healthy, well-watered tree resists most pest problems.

=== Other Care

ESTABLISHMENT SUPPORT FOR AFTERNOON SUN: (1) Shade cloth: Erect a 40–50% shade cloth screen on the west-facing side of the tree (using stakes and garden fabric) during the hottest part of summer (July–August) for the first 2–3 years. This is the single most impactful intervention. (2) Mulch heavily (3–4 inches) from the drip line inward, keeping it away from the trunk — this keeps root zone cooler and retains moisture. (3) Anti-transpirant spray: Apply #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=wilt+pruf+anti+transpirant")[Wilt-Pruf] or similar anti-transpirant to the foliage in early June before peak heat — it forms a temporary protective film reducing moisture loss through leaves. Reapply in July. (4) Avoid reflected heat: check whether a house wall, pavement, or other surface is reflecting additional heat onto the tree — if so, consider a physical barrier or light-colored ground cover nearby. (5) Acidic planting hole: if the tree is ever relocated, amend the new planting zone with 30% acidic peat or pine bark compost to lower local pH. (6) Be patient: Emperor I is a slow-to-establish tree even under good conditions — it may look stressed for 2–4 years before building enough root mass to thrive.
